92186166848 #edb/ref 1039 "Solving for an Unknown Conditional Probability" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166848 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186166698 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166848 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "3a4fee2d-88c3-5381-bd55-98990abc7df3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166877 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186167052 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166877 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186167118 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166877 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186487295 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166877 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186487323 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166877 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186487326 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166877 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186487364 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166877 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186487402 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166877 #edb/ref 1039 "Applying Bayes’ Theorem to a Situation in Context" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166877 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186166482 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166877 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "6c467adb-9d0e-5b33-b81e-4959e2703535" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167047 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186167713 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167047 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186167714 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167047 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186167772 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167047 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186167781 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167047 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167047 #edb/ref 1045 "Extending Bayes' Theorem" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167047 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186167781 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167047 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048270 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167047 #edb/ref 1050 1140.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167047 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "b36ed83a-cc37-5ff2-a68e-2a0e28c0b24e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167117 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186163780 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167117 #edb/ref 1069 "q-147235" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167117 #edb/ref 1071 "The students in a particular school are split among three classes, class $1$, class $2$, and class $3$.\n\n- Class $1$ contains $25%$ of the students in the school, and $55%$ of those students take biology.\n- Class $2$ contains $35%$ of the students in the school, and $40%$ of those students take biology.\n- Class $3$ contains $40%$ of the students in the school.\n\nIf $45%$ of students in the entire school are female, $80%$ of female students take biology, and $70%$ of biology students are female, what is the probability that a randomly selected student from class $3$ takes biology? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places.\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167117 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e39e7e2c-b39e-5302-b92e-8f13f6ffd98c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167117 #edb/ref 1073 true #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167118 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186163786 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167118 #edb/ref 1069 "q-148673" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167118 #edb/ref 1071 "Arthur and Sara are finding the best recipe for chocolate muffins, and they test three different oven cooking modes, mode $1$, mode $2$, and mode $3$. They also try two types of yeast, A and B.\n\n- They use mode $1$ to cook the $30%$ of their muffins, and $90%$ of those muffins turn out well.\n- They use mode $2$ to cook the $40%$ of their muffins, and $85%$ of those muffins turn out well.\n- They use mode $3$ to cook the $30%$ of their muffins, and $88%$ of those muffins turn out well.\n\nIf $20%$ of all muffins was prepared with yeast A, and $87%$ of yeast A muffins turn out well, what is the probability that a randomly chosen good muffin is done with yeast A? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places.\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167118 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "dea495bf-07d9-54cd-baea-bb953539d3dd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167118 #edb/ref 1073 true #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167713 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186167772 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167713 #edb/ref 1061 33287 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167713 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7e6025e7-7c05-542d-a78a-f9d7e627be29" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167713 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186166847 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167714 #edb/ref 1061 33288 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167714 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "fd5da403-f379-5dee-9a50-ecafcf083c36" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167714 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186166848 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167772 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186167714 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167772 #edb/ref 1061 39256 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167772 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7a97606a-98ac-50e6-9e3c-78ae18820b2e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167772 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186166877 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167781 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186167713 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167781 #edb/ref 1061 42168 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167781 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b6bd91e8-6598-5c56-9fa4-bf1144fc830d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167781 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186168143 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168143 #edb/ref 1064 "In this lesson, we will generalize Bayes' theorem to deal with situations where the sample space $\\mathcal S$ is partitioned into a disjoint union of multiple events. \n\nSuppose we have three events $A_1,A_2,$ and $A_3$ that form a disjoint union of the sample space $\\mathcal S,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/89/89a78ef526fcd29a59bacd4d6ae1a456a596d6616af7453f1ba530a610d9a2ac.png)\n\n \n\nNow consider an event $B$ that also lies in the sample space:\n\n![](images/da/daa96423e45f9f80897e2c2d61177eab2faff77f440921866d1b81b7ef2cfe09.png)\n\n \n\nBayes' theorem states that\n\n$$\nP(A_1|B)=\\dfrac{P(B|A_1)P(A_1)}{P(B)}.\n$$\n\nNow, the law of total probability tells us that\n\n$$\nP(B)=P(B|A_1)P(A_1) + P(B|A_2)P(A_2) + P(B|A_3)P(A_3).\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into Bayes' theorem, we get\n\n$$\nP(A_1 | B) = \\dfrac{P(B|A_1) P(A_1)}{P(B|A_1)P(A_1) + P(B|A_2)P(A_2) + P(B|A_3)P(A_3)} .\n$$\n\nUsing similar arguments, we can say that for $A_k$ for $k=1,2,3,$ we have\n\n$$\nP(A_k | B) = \\dfrac{P(B|A_k) P(A_k)}{P(B|A_1)P(A_1) + P(B|A_2)P(A_2) + P(B|A_3)P(A_3)}\n$$\n\nwhich is usually written more compactly as\n\n$$\nP(A_k | B) = \\dfrac{P(B|A_k) P(A_k)}{\\sum_{i=1}^3 P(B|A_i)P(A_i)}.\n$$\n\nIt's easy to see how this generalizes. If the disjoint union $A_1 \\sqcup A_2 \\sqcup \\ldots \\sqcup A_n$ covers the entire sample space, Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability gives us the following:\n\n$$\nP(A_k | B) = \\dfrac{P(B|A_k) P(A_k)}{\\sum_{i=1}^n P(B|A_i)P(A_i)}\n$$\n\nLet's see an example of how to apply this theorem." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168143 #edb/ref 1065 6969 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168143 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "bf17685f-9890-54cc-b8a2-15815558e87a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168143 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
